{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8eea4dbfc5839452","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness gap assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture (SLOs, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n22\t\n23\tTeams are not stated in the strategy (no Impacted Teams table); they are derived\n24\tfrom the architecture-context owning teams: Llama Stack → RHOAI GenAI\n25\t(`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`),\n26\tTrustyAI → TrustyAI (`trustyai.md`), Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard\n27\t(`dashboard.md`).\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness gap assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability and upgrade path]\n34\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 production support posture]\n35\t    E002 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 GA documentation uplift]\n36\t    E003 --> E006\n37\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration] --> E006\n38\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 Dashboard default enablement] --> E006\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The strategy's only open question states the GA scope — and therefore the size and shape of the stability work — is unknown until the assessment lands. The assessment may expand or reshape the API-stability hardening work (true gate, action `rewrite`). |\n46\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The supported configuration matrix and documented scale limits derive from the assessment's scale-limit and error-handling findings; its scope changes with the outcome (true gate, action `rewrite`). |\n47\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | GA documentation describes the stabilized API surface and upgrade path; it is authored after the implementations land. |\n48\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | The docs uplift publishes the support posture (SLOs, backport policy, supported configuration matrix). |\n49\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | The docs uplift documents the guardrails integration as supported. |\n50\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | The docs uplift documents default Dashboard enablement for entitled clusters. |\n51\t| (no edge) E001 → E004 | Rules 14/15 (independent work) | HLR-4 is fully specified in the strategy (promote the existing experimental TrustyAI guardrails integration to supported and documented); the assessment inventories Llama Stack TP quality gaps and does not change whether this integration work exists or what it does. Parallel start. |\n52\t| (no edge) E001 → E005 | Rules 14/15 (independent work) | HLR-5 is fully specified (default enablement for entitled clusters, remove the TP opt-in gate) and owned by a different team on a different component. Parallel start. |\n53\t| (no edge) E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 15 (same component, different areas) | Both are Llama Stack / RHOAI GenAI, but they are different logical changes on different surfaces (API surface and release engineering vs. support-policy authoring and configuration validation) with different review cycles; they run in parallel and coordinate, not serialize. |\n54\t| (no edge) E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | TrustyAI-owned integration work and Dashboard-owned enablement work share no build-against artifact; the Dashboard reads Kubernetes CRs, it does not build against TrustyAI APIs. |\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n57\t\n58\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n59\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n60\t| HLR-1 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment producing the definitive GA scope. |\n61\t| HLR-2 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases. |\n62\t| HLR-3 | P0 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Documented SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix. |\n63\t| HLR-4 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails promoted from experimental flag to supported, documented integration. |\n64\t| HLR-5 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management on by default for entitled clusters. |\n65\t| HLR-6 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Documentation uplift to GA standards including TP migration notes. Priority is P0 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not P2 — see Health Warnings. |\n66\t\n67\tAll six HLRs are covered; no HLR is bundled into another epic's scope, and no\n68\tredundant/overlapping HLR pair exists (each HLR names a distinct capability on a\n69\tdistinct surface).\n70\t\n71\t## Health Warnings\n72\t\n73\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking).** The strategy has no Impacted\n74\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then Acceptance\n75\t   Criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Teams were derived\n76\t   from architecture-context owning teams; epic acceptance criteria were derived\n77\t   from HLR text. A health-check pass on the strategy would strengthen\n78\t   traceability.\n79\t2. **Genuine unknown gating P0 scope.** The strategy's open question — the GA\n80\t   scope is unknown until the HLR-1 assessment lands — makes E002/E003 effort\n81\t   estimates provisional. This is modeled as Investigation E001 with `rewrite`\n82\t   gates on E002/E003. If the assessment reveals gaps beyond the four named\n83\t   dimensions (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling), the\n84\t   GA scope and this decomposition should be revisited.\n85\t3. **Docs epic priority vs. HLR priority.** HLR-6 is P2, but E006 carries P0\n86\t   per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0).\n87\t   Planners should not read E006's P0 as urgency to start it early — it is\n88\t   structurally last (Rule 11) and its priority reflects the strategy-level\n89\t   Critical rating, not the HLR's deferrability.\n90\t\n91\t## Tiered Delivery\n92\t\n93\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing; all\n94\tcomponents are in the platform (architecture-context files exist for Llama\n95\tStack, TrustyAI, and Dashboard) and Konflux-onboarded, so no onboarding chain\n96\tis needed. Partial value flows naturally through parallelism: E004 and E005\n97\tdeliver user-visible value (supported guardrails, default Dashboard\n98\tenablement) independent of the assessment-gated Llama Stack hardening\n99\t(E001 → E002/E003).\n100\t\n101\t## Component Mapping Notes\n102\t\n103\tAll epic `component` values are copied verbatim from\n104\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`: `Llama Stack`, `TrustyAI`, `Dashboard`. The\n105\tdocs-authoring epic (E006) is assigned `Llama Stack` (not the canonical\n106\t`Documentation` component) because its subject matter is the Llama Stack GA\n107\tfeature set and the strategy's Affected Components list names Llama Stack, not\n108\tDocumentation; the RHOAI GenAI team that owns the component drives its docs.\n109\tE004's work spans TrustyAI (integration enablement) and Llama Stack (GA\n110\tdistribution wiring); the single most-relevant canonical component is\n111\t`TrustyAI`, owned by the TrustyAI team, since the deliverable is the guardrails\n112\tintegration becoming supported.\n113\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93cc13bea82195ae","agent_id":"a3c8344ca9bf30bea","session_id":"1137ef5d-6881-4563-aaa6-337da4612527","uuid":"843c2a31-d2f7-4d7b-b49e-3c4745ff361f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:11:58.122Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8eea4dbfc5839452","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
