no experimental disclaimers on the supported path).\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- A Llama Stack distribution can be configured with TrustyAI guardrails\n55\t  through a supported (non-experimental) configuration path.\n56\t- End-to-end verification passes: guardrails are applied to requests on the\n57\t  configured distribution as documented.\n58\t- Integration documentation exists covering configuration, supported\n59\t  deployment shapes, and limitations, with no experimental-status disclaimers\n60\t  on the supported path.\n61\t- The integration is listed as supported for GA distributions in the\n62\t  Llama Stack supported configuration matrix (the\n63\t  support-configuration document delivered by the Llama Stack\n64\t  production support posture workstream) and in this epic's\n65\t  integration documentation.\n66\t- The prior experimental-flag enablement path remains functional\n67\t  through a documented transition window, and the supported\n68\t  configuration path can be reverted or disabled via configuration\n69\t  without code changes (rollback/feature-flag reversibility for the\n70\t  enablement replacement).\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability\n73\t\n74\t- HLR-4 (P1): safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented\n75\t  integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions.\n76\t\n77\t## Risks / Notes\n78\t\n79\t- The work spans TrustyAI (integration enablement, operator surface) and the\n80\t  Llama Stack distribution wiring; the TrustyAI team owns the integration\n81\t  deliverable and coordinates with the Llama Stack owning team on the\n82\t  distribution side.\n83\t- The \"listed as supported\" acceptance criterion depends on the Llama Stack\n84\t  supported configuration matrix, which is authored in parallel by the\n85\t  Llama Stack production support posture workstream (same owning team,\n86\t  different workstream). The TrustyAI team must coordinate its matrix entry\n87\t  with that workstream; the entry lands when